WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Validation asks whether the Bayesian network behaves well enough for its intended purpose.

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.019
Compare predictions with observed outcomes when data is available.

3
00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:20.949
Check probability calibration: when the model says an event has about a 70 percent chance, events in that range should happen roughly 70 percent

4
00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:24.137
of the time over many comparable cases.

5
00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:26.870
Calibration is not about one case.

6
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:30.513
It is about probability quality across repeated use.

7
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:36.434
Sensitivity analysis asks how much outputs change when assumptions or probability values change.

8
00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:43.721
If a small change in one CPT row dramatically changes a decision, that row deserves review.

9
00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:45.998
Test unusual and boundary scenarios.

10
00:00:45.998 --> 00:00:48.730
Review whether important variables are missing.

11
00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:56.928
Check probability sources and document whether they came from data, expert judgment, literature, simulation, or a temporary assumption.

12
00:00:56.928 --> 00:00:58.749
Bayesian networks have limits.

13
00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:01.482
Poor data can produce poor estimates.

14
00:01:01.482 --> 00:01:04.215
Incorrect graph assumptions can distort inference.

15
00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:07.402
Hidden common causes can create misleading relationships.

16
00:01:07.402 --> 00:01:10.590
Rare events may be hard to estimate.

17
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.323
Human probability estimates can be inconsistent.

18
00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:19.699
A model may perform differently for different populations, environments, product versions, or operating conditions.

19
00:01:19.699 --> 00:01:23.798
Outputs can appear more certain than the evidence justifies.

20
00:01:23.798 --> 00:01:25.619
Responsible use requires maintenance.

21
00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:26.986
Track model versions.

22
00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:29.263
Monitor data and environment changes.

23
00:01:29.263 --> 00:01:31.995
Reassess the network when relationships change.

24
00:01:31.995 --> 00:01:37.460
Document purpose, owners, assumptions, limitations, review criteria, missing-data handling, and escalation paths.

25
00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:42.015
For consequential decisions, use human oversight and appropriate domain review.

26
00:01:42.015 --> 00:01:49.301
A Bayesian network can support decision making, but it should not be treated as an oracle.

27
00:01:49.301 --> 00:01:55.222
It is a model whose usefulness depends on fit, evidence, validation, and governance.

28
00:01:55.222 --> 00:01:59.321
Calibration and sensitivity work should be understandable to stakeholders.

29
00:01:59.321 --> 00:02:10.251
A technical team may inspect error metrics and posterior distributions, while a manager may need to know whether the model is reliable enough for

30
00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:11.162
a workflow.

31
00:02:11.162 --> 00:02:12.528
Both views matter.

32
00:02:12.528 --> 00:02:21.636
Responsible reporting should explain where the model performs well, where it is uncertain, and which inputs most affect the result.

33
00:02:21.636 --> 00:02:26.646
That makes the model easier to govern and safer to improve.
